<!-- The Problem -->
<section>
    <h2>The Problem</h2>

    <p>Modern web development has a spacing problem. We've all been there:</p>

    <ul>
        <li>Arbitrary pixel values scattered throughout stylesheets</li>
        <li>Inconsistent spacing that "looks right" but has no underlying logic</li>
        <li>Responsive designs that break at unexpected viewport sizes</li>
        <li>Z-index wars with values climbing into the thousands</li>
        <li>Typography that doesn't scale harmoniously with layout</li>
    </ul>

    <p>Design systems attempt to solve this with spacing tokens like <code>$spacing-sm</code>, <code>$spacing-md</code>,
        <code>$spacing-lg</code>. But these are often arbitrary choices — why is "medium" 16px instead of 14px or 18px?
        Why these breakpoints and not others?</p>

    <p>unit.gl takes a different approach. Instead of arbitrary tokens, we use <strong>mathematical
            relationships</strong> rooted in typography, music theory, and the physical world.</p>
</section>

<!-- The Kyū -->
<section>
    <h2>The Kyū Unit</h2>

    <p>At the heart of unit.gl is the <strong>Kyū</strong> (級, きゅう) — a Japanese unit of measurement traditionally used
        in typography. One Kyū equals exactly 0.25mm, making it one of the smallest practical units in print design.</p>

    <p>In the digital realm, we redefine the Kyū as:</p>

    <div class="api-signature">1Q = 1/16rem = 0.0625rem ≈ 1px (at 16px base font size)</div>

    <p>This isn't arbitrary. The number 16 is fundamental to digital typography:</p>

    <ul>
        <li><strong>16px</strong> is the default browser font size</li>
        <li><strong>16</strong> divides evenly by 2, 4, and 8 (common grid intervals)</li>
        <li><strong>16-point</strong> grids are industry standard in UI design</li>
        <li><strong>16 Q = 1rem</strong> creates a simple mental model</li>
    </ul>

    <p>The beauty of the Q unit is that it provides pixel-level precision while remaining fully scalable. If a user
        increases their browser font size, your entire layout scales proportionally — without breaking.</p>

    <div class="highlight-box">
        <h4>Why "Kyū"?</h4>
        <p>The word 級 (kyū) means "grade," "level," or "rank" in Japanese. It reflects the hierarchical nature of the
            unit — each Q is a building block that combines to create larger structures. The term also connects unit.gl
            to the rich tradition of Japanese typography and its meticulous attention to proportion.</p>
    </div>
</section>

<!-- The Q-System -->
<section>
    <h2>The Q-System</h2>

    <p>Building on the Kyū unit, we developed a complete sizing system based on consistent multipliers:</p>

    <table class="table">
        <thead>
            <tr>
                <th>Level</th>
                <th>Q Value</th>
                <th>Multiplier</th>
                <th>Size (mm)</th>
                <th>Size (px)</th>
            </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr>
                <td><strong>Q0</strong></td>
                <td>1</td>
                <td>×1</td>
                <td>0.25mm</td>
                <td>~1px</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><strong>Q1</strong></td>
                <td>160</td>
                <td>×160</td>
                <td>40mm</td>
                <td>~160px</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><strong>Q2</strong></td>
                <td>320</td>
                <td>×2</td>
                <td>80mm</td>
                <td>~320px</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><strong>Q3</strong></td>
                <td>480</td>
                <td>×3</td>
                <td>120mm</td>
                <td>~480px</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><strong>Q4</strong></td>
                <td>640</td>
                <td>×4</td>
                <td>160mm</td>
                <td>~640px</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><strong>Q5</strong></td>
                <td>800</td>
                <td>×5</td>
                <td>200mm</td>
                <td>~800px</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><strong>Q6</strong></td>
                <td>960</td>
                <td>×6</td>
                <td>240mm</td>
                <td>~960px</td>
            </tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>

    <p>Each Q-level is exactly 160Q (or 40mm) larger than the previous. This creates a perfectly linear scale that's
        easy to reason about and produces harmonious relationships between sizes.</p>
</section>

<!-- 40px Grid -->
<section>
    <h2>The 40px Foundation</h2>

    <p>Why 40? This number appears throughout unit.gl's design:</p>

    <ul>
        <li><strong>40mm = Q1</strong> — The base unit in the Q-system</li>
        <li><strong>40px</strong> — A comfortable touch target on mobile (Apple recommends 44pt minimum)</li>
        <li><strong>40px breakpoints</strong> — All responsive breakpoints are multiples of 40px</li>
        <li><strong>8 × 5 = 40</strong> — Bridges the 8-point grid to larger layout divisions</li>
    </ul>

    <p>By aligning breakpoints to 40px multiples, your layouts naturally snap to grid boundaries at every viewport size:
    </p>

