    <p>These ratios power the <code>modular_scale()</code> function, allowing you to create type scales, spacing
        systems, and layouts that feel inherently harmonious.</p>
</section>

<!-- Swiss Design -->
<section>
    <h2>Swiss Design Influence</h2>

    <p>unit.gl is deeply influenced by the <strong>Swiss International Typographic Style</strong> (also known as the
        International Style), which emerged in the 1950s from the work of designers like Josef Müller-Brockmann, Max
        Bill, and Armin Hofmann.</p>

    <p>Key principles we've adopted:</p>

    <div class="principles-grid">
        <div class="principle">
            <h4>Grid Systems</h4>
            <p>Mathematical grids bring order and consistency. Every element has a precise position determined by the
                underlying structure.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="principle">
            <h4>Objective Typography</h4>
            <p>Type choices based on legibility and function, not decoration. Clear hierarchy through size, weight, and
                space.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="principle">
            <h4>Asymmetric Balance</h4>
            <p>Dynamic compositions that feel balanced without being symmetrical. Tension creates visual interest.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="principle">
            <h4>Reduction</h4>
            <p>Remove everything that doesn't serve a purpose. Every element must justify its existence.</p>
        </div>
    </div>

    <div class="quote">
        <blockquote>
            "The grid system is an aid, not a guarantee. It permits a number of possible uses and each designer can look
            for a solution appropriate to his personal style. But one must learn how to use the grid; it is an art that
            requires practice."
        </blockquote>
        <cite>— Josef Müller-Brockmann</cite>
    </div>
</section>

<!-- Philosophy -->
<section>
    <h2>Design Philosophy</h2>

    <p>unit.gl is built on several core beliefs:</p>

    <div class="philosophy">
        <div class="philosophy__item">
            <h4>Constraints Enable Creativity</h4>
            <p>Working within a system frees you from endless micro-decisions. When spacing, sizing, and proportions
                follow clear rules, you can focus on the problems that actually matter.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="philosophy__item">
            <h4>Mathematics Over Magic Numbers</h4>
            <p>Every value in unit.gl can be traced to a mathematical relationship. No arbitrary choices, no "this looks
                about right." Predictable, reproducible, explainable.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="philosophy__item">
            <h4>Accessibility by Default</h4>
            <p>Using rem-based units means your layouts automatically respect user preferences. If someone needs larger
                text, your entire design scales with them.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="philosophy__item">
            <h4>Print and Screen Unity</h4>
            <p>The Q unit bridges digital and physical media. The same proportional thinking that produces beautiful
                books produces beautiful interfaces.</p>
        </div>
    </div>
</section>

<!-- The Name -->
<section>
    <h2>Why "unit.gl"?</h2>

    <p>The name reflects our focus:</p>

    <ul>
        <li><strong>unit</strong> — The fundamental building blocks of design. Everything starts with the Q.</li>
        <li><strong>.gl</strong> — Originally from Greenland's country code TLD, but for us it evokes "global" and "grid
            layout." It's also satisfyingly short.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>We wanted a name that was memorable, typed easily, and communicated what the library is about at a glance.</p>
</section>

